export const DATA_78: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-115", num: 115, title: "Đề 115", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Inbook: A Bookstore Built on Passion and Community", paragraphs: ["Tucked away on a quiet street in Hanoi's Tay Ho district, Inbook is not the kind of bookstore that announces itself loudly. There is no neon signage, no towering display of bestsellers facing the pavement, and no automated greeting when you step through the door. What greets visitors instead is the smell of paper and wood, the soft murmur of a fan turning overhead, and the sight of Lan Phuong herself — usually seated behind a low wooden counter, reading, marking passages, or writing notes in the margins of whatever book she is currently working through. For the regulars who have found their way to Inbook over the years, this quiet, unhurried atmosphere is precisely the point.", "Lan Phuong opened Inbook in 2018 after more than a decade working as an editor at a mid-sized publishing house in Hanoi. The decision to leave a stable job and open an independent bookstore was, by her own account, not a carefully calculated business move. \"I just wanted a place where books could be taken seriously,\" she has said in interviews, \"not as products to be moved off shelves as quickly as possible, but as things worth sitting with.\" The store's name — a contraction of \"into books\" — reflects this philosophy: the idea that reading is not a transaction but an immersion , and that a good bookstore should facilitate that immersion rather than simply accelerate consumption.", "The physical space of Inbook is modest by any commercial standard, occupying a narrow two-story shophouse of approximately 60 square meters. Yet the use of that space is deliberate and considered. The ground floor houses fiction, poetry, and translated literature arranged not alphabetically or by genre but according to a system that Lan Phuong describes as \"thematic proximity\" — books placed near other books they are in conversation with, regardless of their author or country of origin. A novel set in wartime Vietnam might sit beside a memoir from Bosnia and a collection of essays on memory and loss. The arrangement rewards browsing and frequently surprises even customers who believe they already know what they are looking for.", "Upstairs, a small reading room with four low tables and cushioned floor seating functions as an informal gathering space for the book clubs, author talks, and reading circles that Lan Phuong organizes on a roughly monthly basis. These events are kept deliberately small — rarely more than fifteen to twenty participants — and are structured around close reading and extended discussion rather than the promotional format typical of larger literary festivals. Authors who have spoken at Inbook have included both established Vietnamese writers and younger debut novelists, several of whom have mentioned in subsequent interviews that the intimate format of the Inbook sessions allowed for a depth of conversation about their work that larger venues rarely permit.", "Financially, the bookstore operates on margins that Lan Phuong openly acknowledges are thin. Independent bookstores in Vietnam, as in many other countries, face considerable pressure from online retailers who can offer deep discounts that physical stores cannot match without absorbing losses. Inbook's response to this pressure has not been to compete on price but to offer something that online retail structurally cannot: a curated , personally selected inventory in which every title has been read or at minimum seriously evaluated by Lan Phuong herself before being stocked. Regular customers describe browsing Inbook as a form of guided discovery — the sense that the person who chose these books has a coherent vision of what literature can do, and that following her choices leads somewhere worthwhile.", "Beyond its role as a retail space, Inbook has gradually become a small but recognizable node in Hanoi's independent literary culture. Lan Phuong maintains a monthly newsletter in which she discusses books she has been reading, translations she considers undervalued, and occasional reflections on the experience of running an independent bookstore in a commercial environment that is, at best, indifferent to its survival. The newsletter, which has several thousand subscribers despite never having been formally advertised, functions as an extension of the bookstore's curatorial voice — a way of maintaining a conversation with readers who may visit Inbook only occasionally but who remain engaged with the sensibility it represents.", "Whether Inbook can sustain itself in the long term remains, in Lan Phuong's own telling, an open question. She has resisted the temptation to expand — to open a second location, to introduce a cafe component, or to develop the kind of merchandise and event programming that has allowed some independent bookstores in other cities to diversify their revenue streams. Her reasoning is straightforward: growth, in her view, would change the nature of what Inbook is. \"If I made it bigger, I would have to make it less,\" she has said. For now, the store continues as it began — small, selective, and sustained by the conviction that there is a community of readers, however modest in number, for whom a bookstore like this is not a luxury but a necessity."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of this passage?", options: ["To compare Inbook's business model with those of larger commercial bookstores in Hanoi and evaluate which approach is more financially sustainable", "To trace Lan Phuong's career from her years as a publishing editor through to her decision to open one of Hanoi's most talked-about literary venues", "To argue that independent bookstores in Vietnam need government support to survive the growing competition from online retailers offering deep discounts", "To profile Inbook as an independent bookstore defined by a coherent reading philosophy, community engagement, and deliberate resistance to commercial expansion"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"this\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The contraction of the words \"into books\" that Lan Phuong chose as the name for her bookstore to reflect its purpose and identity", "Lan Phuong's decision to leave a stable editorial career at a publishing house in order to open her own independent bookstore in Hanoi", "The commercially cautious approach Lan Phuong adopted when calculating the risks of opening a small retail business without external financial backing", "The philosophy that reading is an immersion rather than a transaction, and that a bookstore should facilitate that experience rather than simply accelerate consumption"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"These\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["The four low tables and cushioned floor seats that furnish the small reading room on the upper floor of the Inbook shophouse", "The larger literary festivals that typically use a promotional format which Lan Phuong has deliberately chosen not to replicate at Inbook", "The established Vietnamese writers and younger debut novelists who have spoken at Inbook and later mentioned it in interviews", "The book clubs, author talks, and reading circles that Lan Phuong organizes on a roughly monthly basis in the upstairs reading room"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does Lan Phuong organize the books on the ground floor of Inbook?", options: ["Books are placed according to thematic proximity — near other books they are in conversation with — regardless of author or country of origin", "Books are arranged alphabetically by author surname within broad genre categories such as fiction, poetry, and translated literature", "Books are displayed in the order they were acquired by the store, with newer arrivals placed at the front and older stock moved to the rear", "Books are grouped by country of origin, so that Vietnamese literature occupies one section and international titles are sorted by language"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does Inbook respond to competitive pressure from online retailers?", options: ["By periodically offering discount promotions on selected titles that allow the store to match the prices available through major online platforms", "By offering a personally curated inventory in which every title has been read or carefully evaluated by Lan Phuong before being stocked", "By partnering with publishing houses to stock advance copies of new releases before they become available through other retail channels", "By stocking exclusively Vietnamese-language titles that are not easily available through international online retail platforms"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is true about Lan Phuong's monthly newsletter?", options: ["It is distributed in print form to regular customers visiting the store and also made available digitally to an international audience", "It focuses exclusively on reviews of newly published Vietnamese literature and announcements of upcoming Inbook events", "It has attracted several thousand subscribers despite never having been promoted through any formal advertising campaign", "It was launched as a paid subscription service to provide Inbook with a secondary income stream independent of book sales revenue"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"immersion\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["A commercial transaction in which a reader exchanges money for a book and expects a straightforward and efficient service experience", "The state of being deeply and completely absorbed in an activity, with full attention directed toward it rather than divided among competing demands", "A formal educational method in which students are surrounded by material in a target language or subject area to accelerate their learning", "The gradual accumulation of knowledge about a subject through repeated short encounters spread across an extended period of time"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"curated\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Carefully and deliberately selected according to a coherent vision, with each item chosen for a specific purpose rather than stocked at random", "Imported directly, meaning that the titles stocked at Inbook are sourced from publishers overseas rather than through domestic distributors", "Priced competitively, meaning that each item in the inventory has been assessed against market rates to ensure value for the customer", "Formally catalogued, meaning that every title in the store has been assigned a classification code and entered into a database for easy retrieval"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a form of expansion that Lan Phuong has deliberately resisted?", options: ["Opening a second Inbook location in another part of Hanoi or in a different city", "Introducing a cafe component to the existing shophouse to generate additional revenue", "Launching a lending library or book rental service to attract readers who cannot afford to purchase books outright", "Developing merchandise and event programming to diversify the store's income streams"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the long-term foundation of Inbook's survival?", options: ["Inbook's survival depends primarily on Lan Phuong securing external investment that would allow the store to reduce its reliance on book sales alone", "The store's future is most secure if Lan Phuong transitions from physical retail to an online platform that can reach readers beyond Hanoi's Tay Ho district", "Inbook's long-term viability requires that Lan Phuong eventually expand the store's offerings to attract a wider and more commercially mainstream audience", "The store's continued existence rests on maintaining a small but committed community of readers for whom it serves an essential rather than optional purpose"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Building Blocks of Music: Notes, Pitch, Rhythm, and Beyond", paragraphs: ["Music is often described as a universal language, yet like any language, it is built from a system of discrete, learnable elements that combine according to both rules and creative conventions. For listeners who engage with music primarily through emotional experience, the structural foundations of musical sound can seem invisible — present in every piece yet rarely examined consciously. Understanding these foundations does not diminish the experience of music; if anything, it deepens it, offering a richer vocabulary for articulating why certain combinations of sounds produce the responses they do. The most fundamental of these building blocks are the note, pitch, rhythm, dynamics, and timbre — elements that interact in every musical performance, from the simplest folk melody to the most complex orchestral composition.", "A note is the basic unit of musical sound: a discrete event defined by its pitch, duration, and, in the context of performance, its volume and the quality of the sound producing it. In Western music notation, notes are represented as symbols placed on a staff — a set of five horizontal lines — and their position on the staff indicates their pitch while their shape conveys their duration. The note middle C, for instance, occupies a specific position on the staff and sounds at a frequency of approximately 261.63 hertz (Hz), meaning the sound wave it produces completes 261.63 cycles per second. Notes that are not being played are represented by rests, which indicate periods of deliberate silence — elements as structurally significant to music as the notes themselves .", "Pitch refers to the perceived highness or lowness of a sound, a quality determined by the frequency of the sound wave it generates. Higher frequencies produce higher pitches; lower frequencies produce lower ones. In Western music, pitches are organized into a system of twelve distinct notes within each octave — an interval spanning a doubling of frequency — with the pattern repeating across multiple octaves in both directions. These twelve pitches, arranged in ascending or descending order, constitute the chromatic scale, from which all other scales — major, minor, pentatonic, and others — are derived by selecting specific subsets of notes according to defined patterns of intervals. The ability to perceive and reproduce pitch accurately is central to virtually all musical traditions, though the precise organization of pitch into scales and tuning systems varies significantly across cultures.", "Rhythm describes the organization of musical events in time, governing when notes begin and end and how they relate to one another within the flow of a piece. The basic rhythmic framework of most music is the beat — a regular, recurring pulse that provides a temporal reference point for all other musical events. Beats are grouped into measures or bars, each containing a fixed number of beats as specified by the time signature at the beginning of a piece. A time signature of 4/4, the most common in Western popular and classical music, indicates four beats per measure, with the quarter note receiving one beat. Syncopation — the deliberate placement of emphasis on beats or parts of beats that are not normally stressed — is one of the most important rhythmic devices in jazz, funk, and many other genres, creating rhythmic tension and energy that straightforward on-beat patterns do not produce.", "Dynamics refer to the variation in loudness or softness of musical sound, and they constitute one of the most expressive dimensions available to performers and composers alike. In standard Western music notation, dynamics are indicated by Italian terms and their corresponding abbreviations: pianissimo (pp) for very soft, piano (p) for soft, mezzo-piano (mp) for moderately soft, mezzo-forte (mf) for moderately loud, forte (f) for loud, and fortissimo (ff) for very loud. Beyond these fixed markings, composers may indicate gradual changes in dynamic level through the terms crescendo (gradually getting louder) and decrescendo or diminuendo (gradually getting softer). Skilled performers do not treat dynamic markings as absolute instructions but as interpretive guides, shaping the dynamic contour of a piece in response to its emotional arc and the acoustic properties of the performance space.", "Timbre — sometimes called tone color — refers to the distinctive quality of a sound that allows listeners to distinguish between different instruments or voices producing the same pitch at the same volume. It is timbre that makes a violin playing middle C sound different from a clarinet or a human voice producing the same note. Timbre is determined by the harmonic series produced alongside the fundamental frequency: most musical sounds consist not of a single pure frequency but of a complex mixture of the fundamental pitch and a series of overtones, or harmonics, at higher frequencies. The specific pattern and relative strength of these overtones is what gives each instrument or voice its characteristic sound quality. Advances in digital audio technology have made it possible to analyze and synthesize timbres with considerable precision, leading to the development of electronic instruments capable of convincingly replicating the sound of acoustic ones.", "These five elements — note, pitch, rhythm, dynamics, and timbre — do not exist in isolation within any actual piece of music. They interact continuously, and it is the nature of their interaction that produces the experience of music as a meaningful, expressive, and emotionally resonant phenomenon. A melody is not simply a sequence of pitches but a sequence in which pitch, rhythm, dynamics, and timbre combine to create a particular quality of movement through time. Harmony arises when multiple pitches sound simultaneously, producing combinations that can range from stable and consonant to tense and dissonant, and whose resolution or lack of resolution drives the emotional narrative of a piece forward. Understanding these elements and their interactions is not a substitute for musical experience but a means of making that experience more conscious, more articulable, and ultimately more fully realized."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of this passage?", options: ["To explain the fundamental elements that constitute musical sound and demonstrate how their interaction produces meaningful musical experience", "To trace the historical development of Western music notation from its earliest forms to the standardized system used by composers and performers today", "To argue that listeners who lack formal music theory training are unable to fully appreciate the emotional and structural complexity of musical compositions", "To compare Western music theory with musical traditions from other cultures and evaluate which system offers the most complete framework for understanding sound"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"themselves\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The five horizontal lines of the musical staff on which both notes and rests are placed to indicate their pitch and duration", "The periods of deliberate silence in a musical piece, which are represented by rests and carry equal structural importance to sounding notes", "The symbols used in Western music notation to represent both notes and rests on the staff in a standardized visual system", "The notes of the musical scale, which the passage has just used as a point of comparison to emphasize the structural significance of rests"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the relationship between the chromatic scale and other musical scales?", options: ["The chromatic scale and major or minor scales are entirely independent systems, each used by different musical traditions with no structural relationship between them", "Other scales such as major, minor, and pentatonic are derived from the chromatic scale by selecting specific subsets of its twelve notes according to defined interval patterns", "Major and minor scales must be learned before the chromatic scale, because the chromatic scale combines elements of all other scales into a single advanced sequence", "The chromatic scale is a simplified version of major and minor scales, designed to introduce beginners to pitch organization before they progress to more complex systems"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does a time signature of 4/4 indicate in a musical piece?", options: ["That there are four measures in each section of the piece, and each measure must be separated by a brief pause of one quarter beat", "That the piece alternates between four loud beats and four soft beats within each measure, creating a regular dynamic pattern throughout", "That the piece should be played at a tempo of four beats per minute, with each beat receiving exactly one quarter of the total duration", "That each measure contains four beats and the quarter note receives one beat, making it the most common time signature in Western music"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what effect does syncopation produce in musical performance?", options: ["It slows the perceived tempo of a piece by placing deliberate pauses between the regular beats, creating a sense of suspension and anticipation", "It creates rhythmic tension and energy by placing emphasis on beats or parts of beats that are not normally stressed in a standard rhythmic pattern", "It eliminates the need for a regular beat entirely, allowing performers in jazz and funk to improvise freely without reference to a fixed temporal framework", "It reinforces the natural stress patterns of the beat by emphasizing the strongest beats in each measure, producing a driving and predictable rhythmic feel"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what physical characteristic of a musical sound determines its timbre?", options: ["The volume at which the sound is produced, since instruments played at higher dynamic levels generate a richer and more complex mixture of frequencies", "The physical size of the instrument producing the sound, since larger instruments always generate lower fundamental frequencies and a broader harmonic range", "The speed at which the performer produces the sound, which affects the rate of vibration and alters the quality of the tone heard by the listener", "The specific pattern and relative strength of overtones produced alongside the fundamental frequency, which give each instrument its characteristic sound quality"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"pianissimo\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["At a very high volume, indicating the loudest dynamic level that a performer is expected to reach during a particular passage of music", "With a gradual increase in volume, instructing the performer to move progressively from a quiet level toward a louder one over several measures", "At a very soft volume, representing one of the quietest dynamic levels indicated in standard Western music notation", "At a moderate volume that falls between the soft and loud extremes of the dynamic range available to performers in a given musical context"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"synthesize\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["To record with high fidelity, meaning to capture the acoustic properties of a live instrument in a digital format without any alteration to the original sound", "To analyze in detail, meaning to break a complex sound down into its component frequencies in order to understand its underlying acoustic structure", "To amplify electronically, meaning to increase the volume and projection of a naturally quiet sound so that it can be heard in a larger performance space", "To produce or construct artificially, meaning to generate a sound or tonal quality through electronic means rather than through a physical acoustic instrument"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT identified as one of the five fundamental building blocks of music discussed in the passage?", options: ["Timbre, which refers to the distinctive tone color of a sound that allows listeners to distinguish between different instruments at the same pitch", "Dynamics, which describe the variation in loudness or softness of musical sound and constitute a key expressive dimension for performers", "Harmony, which arises when multiple pitches sound simultaneously and produces combinations ranging from consonant to dissonant", "Pitch, which refers to the perceived highness or lowness of a sound as determined by the frequency of the sound wave it generates"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the relationship between theoretical knowledge of music and the emotional experience of listening to it?", options: ["The emotional experience of music is entirely independent of theoretical understanding, since most listeners engage with music without any awareness of its structural elements", "Theoretical knowledge inevitably reduces music to an abstract analytical exercise that distances listeners from the spontaneous emotional responses it would otherwise produce", "Formal theoretical training is necessary before a listener can meaningfully appreciate complex musical forms, since untrained listeners lack the cognitive framework to process them", "Understanding the structural elements of music and experiencing it emotionally are complementary rather than opposing — theoretical knowledge can deepen and enrich musical experience"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Algorithmic Turn: Recommendation Systems and the Reshaping of Public Discourse", paragraphs: ["Few technological developments of the past two decades have reshaped the experience of information consumption as profoundly as the recommendation algorithm. Deployed across social media platforms, video streaming services, news aggregators, and e-commerce sites, recommendation systems use machine learning techniques to analyze user behavior — clicks, viewing duration, search history, and interaction patterns — and generate personalized feeds of content predicted to maximize continued engagement. For the platforms that operate them, these systems have proven extraordinarily effective at their stated objective: users exposed to algorithmically curated content spend measurably more time on platform than those navigating manually curated or chronologically ordered feeds. What remains far more contested is what this effectiveness costs, and who bears the cost.", "The technical architecture underlying most contemporary recommendation algorithms is built around a concept known as collaborative filtering. Rather than evaluating the intrinsic qualities of content — its accuracy, depth, or civic relevance — collaborative filtering identifies users whose past behavior resembles that of the target user and recommends content that those similar users have engaged with. The result is a system that optimizes for behavioral similarity rather than informational quality: content that triggers rapid emotional responses, particularly outrage, curiosity, or anxiety, tends to generate higher engagement signals than content that requires sustained attention or nuanced consideration, and is therefore systematically amplified regardless of its veracity or public value.", "This dynamic has given rise to what researchers have termed the \"filter bubble\" — a condition in which algorithmic personalization progressively narrows the informational environment of individual users, exposing them predominantly to content that confirms pre-existing beliefs and preferences while reducing their encounter with challenging or contradictory perspectives. [1] The filter bubble hypothesis, first articulated by internet activist Eli Pariser in 2011, has generated substantial empirical debate: some studies have found strong evidence of ideological sorting driven by algorithmic recommendation, while others suggest that algorithmic systems expose users to more diverse content than they would select for themselves through manual browsing. The methodological challenges of measuring exposure, engagement, and belief formation across algorithmically governed information environments remain significant, and no academic consensus has yet emerged.", "Beyond the individual level, critics argue that recommendation algorithms have had systemic effects on public discourse that extend well beyond the browsing habits of individual users. When large proportions of a population receive algorithmically mediated information, the aggregate effect can be a fragmentation of shared informational reality — a condition in which citizens inhabiting the same political community may be exposed to substantially different facts, framings, and interpretations of the same events. This fragmentation, some scholars argue, undermines the epistemic foundations necessary for democratic deliberation: productive public debate requires not only freedom of expression but a sufficient degree of shared factual common ground from which disagreements can be meaningfully adjudicated. [2]", "The platforms operating these systems have not been indifferent to these critiques, though the nature and sincerity of their responses remain subjects of considerable skepticism. Following widespread criticism of the role played by social media algorithms in the spread of health misinformation during the COVID-19 pandemic, several major platforms introduced modifications to their recommendation systems designed to reduce the amplification of content flagged by independent fact-checkers. YouTube announced adjustments intended to limit recommendations of content classified as \"borderline\" — material that does not violate platform policies outright but that approaches the boundaries of misinformation or harmful content. [3] Independent audits of these changes have produced mixed findings, with some researchers documenting meaningful reductions in the recommendation of extreme content and others concluding that the modifications were largely cosmetic and that engagement-maximizing logic continued to govern recommendation outcomes at the system level.", "Regulatory responses to algorithmic recommendation systems have developed unevenly across jurisdictions. The European Union's Digital Services Act, which entered into force in 2023, represents the most comprehensive legislative framework yet developed for governing the operation of large online platforms, including requirements for transparency about how recommendation systems function, the option for users to access non-personalized feeds, and mandatory risk assessments for systemic platforms whose algorithmic outputs may have significant societal effects. In the United States, by contrast, regulatory action has been constrained by legal frameworks that provide broad immunity to platforms for third-party content and by a political environment in which concerns about algorithmic harm have not yet translated into durable legislative consensus. [4]", "The deeper challenge posed by recommendation algorithms may be less technical than structural. The engagement-maximizing objective function that drives these systems is not an accidental design choice but a direct consequence of the advertising-based business model on which most major platforms depend: user attention is the commodity being sold, and algorithms are the mechanism for harvesting it at scale. Reforming algorithmic recommendation in ways that meaningfully prioritize informational quality, civic value, or user autonomy over engagement metrics would require either a fundamental restructuring of platform business models or the imposition of regulatory requirements that mandate alternative optimization objectives — neither of which is likely to occur rapidly or without significant resistance from the commercial interests involved."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of this passage?", options: ["To argue that social media platforms have deliberately engineered recommendation systems to spread political misinformation for commercial gain", "To evaluate whether the European Union's Digital Services Act represents a sufficient regulatory response to the harms caused by algorithmic recommendation systems", "To compare the technical design of collaborative filtering systems with alternative recommendation approaches and assess which produces better informational outcomes", "To examine how recommendation algorithms function, their effects on individual users and public discourse, and the structural obstacles to meaningful reform"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The online platforms that deploy collaborative filtering systems to analyze user behavior and maximize continued engagement with their services", "The content items that have been flagged by independent fact-checkers as likely to trigger emotional responses such as outrage or anxiety", "The users whose past behavior resembles that of the target user and whose engagement history informs what content the system recommends", "The recommendation algorithms themselves, which evaluate the engagement patterns of all users before generating personalized content feeds"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"undermines\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["Redirects systematically, suggesting that algorithmic systems channel public debate away from contested political questions toward topics of broader consensus", "Strengthens gradually, suggesting that algorithmic fragmentation of information inadvertently creates new incentives for citizens to seek out shared factual frameworks", "Measures precisely, suggesting that the degree of epistemic damage caused by algorithmic recommendation can be quantified through systematic research methods", "Weakens or erodes, suggesting that informational fragmentation damages the shared factual foundation that democratic deliberation depends upon"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"cosmetic\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Commercially motivated, meaning that the platform changes were introduced not to reduce harm but to generate positive publicity and improve advertiser confidence", "Scientifically rigorous, meaning that the platform changes were developed through controlled experimental testing before being deployed to the wider user population", "Visually appealing, meaning that the platform changes were designed primarily to improve the aesthetic appearance of the user interface rather than its underlying logic", "Superficial and concerned only with outward appearance, meaning that the modifications did not alter the fundamental engagement-maximizing logic of the recommendation system"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the fundamental principle on which collaborative filtering recommendation systems operate?", options: ["They analyze the emotional language used in content and prioritize material that is likely to generate a calm, sustained reading experience", "They identify users with similar behavioral patterns and recommend to the target user content that those similar users have previously engaged with", "They evaluate the factual accuracy and civic relevance of each piece of content before deciding whether to recommend it to a given user", "They combine editorial judgment from human curators with automated behavioral data to ensure that recommended content meets minimum quality thresholds"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is a requirement introduced by the European Union's Digital Services Act?", options: ["All recommendation systems operating in the EU must be independently certified as free from bias before they are permitted to process user data", "Large platforms must provide transparency about how their recommendation systems work and offer users the option of a non-personalized feed", "Platforms are required to allocate a fixed percentage of their advertising revenue to funding independent research on algorithmic harm", "Platforms must obtain explicit consent from users before deploying any form of personalized recommendation algorithm on their services"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how did YouTube respond to criticism of its recommendation system following the COVID-19 pandemic?", options: ["YouTube announced adjustments intended to limit recommendations of borderline content — material approaching but not violating platform policies on misinformation", "YouTube introduced a mandatory delay before any health-related content could be recommended, allowing independent fact-checkers to review it first", "YouTube replaced its engagement-based recommendation algorithm entirely with an editorially curated system vetted by independent public health authorities", "YouTube removed its recommendation system from health and science categories entirely, reverting to chronologically ordered feeds for those content areas"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a consequence of algorithmic recommendation systems on society or individuals?", options: ["The fragmentation of shared informational reality among citizens who may be exposed to substantially different facts and framings of the same events", "A measurable decline in overall internet usage as users become frustrated with the repetitive nature of algorithmically personalized content feeds", "The progressive narrowing of users' informational environments toward content that confirms their existing beliefs and preferences", "The systematic amplification of content that triggers rapid emotional responses regardless of its accuracy or public value"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Critics further note that this sorting effect is self-reinforcing: the more a user engages with a particular type of content, the more the algorithm amplifies similar content, deepening the bubble over time.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 7 about the likelihood of meaningful reform to algorithmic recommendation systems in the near future?", options: ["Reform will be driven by user demand once the harms of algorithmic recommendation become sufficiently visible and users begin migrating to platforms with alternative recommendation models", "Reform is likely to occur rapidly once regulatory frameworks like the EU's Digital Services Act demonstrate their effectiveness and are adopted by other jurisdictions", "The primary obstacle to reform is technical rather than commercial — current machine learning methods are simply not capable of optimizing for informational quality rather than engagement", "Meaningful reform is unlikely in the short term because the engagement-maximizing logic of these systems is structurally tied to advertising-based business models that platforms are strongly incentivized to protect"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Repatriation Debate: Cultural Heritage, Colonial History, and the Ethics of Museum Ownership", paragraphs: ["Few questions in contemporary cultural policy have generated as much sustained controversy as the repatriation of cultural heritage objects — the return of artifacts, artworks, human remains, and sacred objects held in Western museums and private collections to the communities, nations, or peoples from whom they were originally removed. The debate sits at the intersection of international law, postcolonial ethics, museum theory, and nationalist politics, and it resists resolution along any single axis of argument. What makes the repatriation question genuinely difficult — as opposed to merely politically contentious — is that it involves competing claims of approximately equal moral seriousness, each grounded in defensible principles, and no established framework for adjudicating between them that commands broad cross-cultural assent.", "The case for repatriation is grounded primarily in the history of how most significant collections of non-Western cultural objects came to reside in European and North American institutions. The overwhelming majority of these objects were acquired during the colonial period through mechanisms that ranged from outright military seizure and punitive expedition to purchase under conditions of severe economic and political coercion that render the concept of free consent largely fictitious . The Benin Bronzes, looted from the Kingdom of Benin by a British military expedition in 1897; the Elgin Marbles, removed from the Parthenon in Athens under contested Ottoman permission in the early nineteenth century; the Nefertiti bust, taken from Egypt under a disputed export license in 1913 — each represents a category of acquisition whose ethical legitimacy has become increasingly difficult to defend as postcolonial scholarship has reconstructed the conditions under which these transfers occurred.", "Proponents of repatriation argue, further, that the continued retention of these objects perpetuates a relationship of cultural dispossession that was itself a constitutive element of colonial domination. Sacred objects removed from indigenous communities and displayed in natural history museums, ancestral human remains held in anthropological collections, and ceremonial artifacts whose ritual function depends on their presence within the communities that created and used them — these categories of object carry dimensions of meaning and relationship that cannot be adequately honored within the display conventions of a Western encyclopedic museum, however sensitively curated. [1] The argument, in its strongest form, is not merely that these communities would prefer to have their objects back, but that the absence of these objects constitutes an ongoing harm that the original act of removal initiated and that continued retention perpetuates.", "The counter-arguments advanced by major holding institutions are neither trivial nor purely self-interested, though they are frequently characterized as such by repatriation advocates. The most substantive of these arguments concerns the universalist conception of the museum as a site of human cultural heritage available to all of humanity rather than belonging to any single national or cultural community. In this view, articulated most explicitly in the 1954 Declaration on the Importance and Value of Universal Museums signed by nineteen major institutions, objects held in encyclopedic collections serve an educational and civilizational function that transcends their cultural origin — making them available to audiences worldwide who would not otherwise have access to the full breadth of human creative achievement. To return an object to its country of origin, this argument holds, is to prioritize the claims of one community over the legitimate interests of the global public that museum collections are designed to serve.", "A related institutional concern involves the question of preservation and stewardship. Major Western museums can point to substantial investments in conservation infrastructure, climate-controlled storage, and technical expertise that, in some cases, genuinely exceed the resources currently available to institutions in origin countries. The argument that objects are better preserved where they currently reside has, however, been significantly weakened over the past three decades by the development of world-class museum infrastructure in many formerly colonized nations and by the growing recognition that the preservation argument has historically been deployed selectively and in bad faith — invoked to justify retention in cases where no genuine conservation concern exists and abandoned when it does not serve institutional interests. [2]", "The legal landscape governing repatriation is complex and remains weighted substantially in favor of holding institutions. The 1970 UNESCO Convention on the Means of Prohibiting and Preventing the Illicit Import, Export and Transfer of Ownership of Cultural Property established a widely ratified international framework for combating the illicit trade in cultural objects, but its non-retroactive character means that it provides no legal basis for claims against objects acquired before its adoption — the period that encompasses the vast majority of contested colonial acquisitions. [3] National legislation in holding countries has generally been interpreted to prohibit the deaccessioning of objects from national museum collections, creating an additional structural barrier to repatriation even where political will exists. Some bilateral agreements between specific countries have produced notable returns — Germany's repatriation of Benin Bronzes to Nigeria beginning in 2022 being among the most significant recent examples — but these agreements remain exceptional rather than systemic.", "Emerging frameworks for addressing the repatriation question have increasingly moved beyond the binary of full repatriation versus permanent retention toward more nuanced arrangements: long-term loans of contested objects to origin communities while legal title remains with the holding institution, joint stewardship agreements that distribute both ownership and curatorial responsibility, and collaborative exhibition projects that involve origin communities in the interpretation of their own cultural heritage. [4] These approaches have been welcomed by some advocates as pragmatic steps toward redress and criticized by others as mechanisms that preserve institutional control under a rhetorical veneer of partnership. What is clear is that the repatriation debate has irrevocably altered the terms on which major cultural institutions must now justify their collections — and that the encyclopedic museum of the twentieth century, organized around assumptions of universal custodianship that postcolonial scholarship has rendered untenable, is undergoing a fundamental renegotiation of its relationship with the communities whose heritage it holds.", "The stakes of this renegotiation extend beyond any individual object or claim. How societies resolve questions of cultural ownership, historical accountability, and institutional authority over the past will shape not only the collections of major museums but the broader terms on which postcolonial relationships between nations and peoples are conducted. The museum, in this sense, has become an unlikely but consequential arena for working through some of the deepest unresolved tensions of the modern world — and the artifacts at the center of the repatriation debate are not merely objects of aesthetic or historical interest but material evidence of histories that remain, for many communities, painfully and consequentially unfinished."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best represents the central argument of the passage?", options: ["That the legal barriers to repatriation are so significant that meaningful reform can only be achieved through a new binding international treaty replacing the inadequate UNESCO Convention of 1970", "That the case for repatriation of colonial-era cultural objects is morally overwhelming and that holding institutions have no legitimate grounds for continued retention", "That emerging compromise frameworks such as long-term loans and joint stewardship represent the most practical and ethically defensible resolution to the repatriation question", "That the repatriation debate involves competing claims of genuine moral seriousness and is driving a fundamental renegotiation of the relationship between major museums and the communities whose heritage they hold"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"fictitious\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Historically documented, meaning that the records of consent were fabricated after the fact by colonial administrations to legitimize transfers that had already occurred", "Not genuine or real, meaning that the conditions of severe coercion under which consent was given render it meaningless as an expression of free agreement", "Legally binding, meaning that the consent given by colonized peoples to the transfer of their cultural objects was recognized as valid under the international law of the period", "Strategically negotiated, meaning that colonial powers and local authorities reached formal agreements about the transfer of objects through diplomatic channels"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"deaccessioning\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["The conservation technique by which deteriorating objects are stabilized and prepared for long-term storage in climate-controlled museum facilities", "The legal mechanism by which a national government designates specific cultural objects as protected heritage that cannot be exported or transferred", "The formal process by which a museum acquires new objects for its permanent collection through purchase, gift, or bequest from donors and estates", "The administrative procedure by which a museum removes an object from its permanent collection, typically in order to sell, transfer, or return it"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["The origin communities whose cultural objects are held in Western museums and who are positioned as one constituency among many in the universalist framework", "The global audiences who visit encyclopedic museum collections and would not otherwise encounter the full breadth of human creative achievement", "The major holding institutions that signed the 1954 Declaration on the Importance and Value of Universal Museums", "The objects held in encyclopedic collections, which the universalist argument holds should be available to all of humanity rather than any single community"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the strongest form of the argument made by repatriation proponents regarding communities and their missing objects?", options: ["That the continued retention of cultural objects in Western museums prevents origin communities from developing their own museum infrastructure and curatorial expertise", "That objects held in encyclopedic museums are systematically misrepresented and decontextualized in ways that distort the cultural identity of the communities that created them", "That the absence of these objects constitutes an ongoing harm that the original act of removal initiated and that continued retention by holding institutions perpetuates", "That origin communities deserve financial compensation proportional to the cultural and economic value that holding institutions have derived from displaying their objects"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is a significant limitation of the 1970 UNESCO Convention in the context of colonial-era repatriation claims?", options: ["It was ratified by only a small number of holding countries and therefore lacks the broad international authority necessary to compel compliance from major museums", "Its non-retroactive character means it provides no legal basis for claims involving objects acquired before 1970, which includes the vast majority of contested colonial acquisitions", "Its enforcement mechanisms depend entirely on the goodwill of holding institutions and cannot be applied to privately owned collections or objects held outside national museums", "It focuses exclusively on preventing future illicit trade and does not address the question of how origin communities should be defined or represented in repatriation proceedings"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why has the preservation argument for retaining objects in Western museums been significantly weakened in recent decades?", options: ["Legal rulings in several European jurisdictions have established that the preservation argument cannot be invoked to override the cultural ownership rights of origin communities", "International surveys have found that the conservation standards maintained by major Western museums have declined substantially due to funding cuts and institutional neglect", "Scientific advances in digital reproduction technology have made it possible to create near-perfect replicas of cultural objects, eliminating the need to hold originals in any single location", "The development of world-class museum infrastructure in formerly colonized nations and recognition that the preservation argument has been deployed selectively and in bad faith have undermined its credibility"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a specific example of a contested colonial acquisition discussed in the passage?", options: ["The Elgin Marbles, removed from the Parthenon in Athens under contested Ottoman permission in the early nineteenth century", "The Rosetta Stone, taken from Egypt by French forces during the Napoleonic campaign and subsequently acquired by the British Museum", "The Benin Bronzes, looted from the Kingdom of Benin by a British military expedition in 1897", "The Nefertiti bust, taken from Egypt under a disputed export license in 1913"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This is particularly evident in the case of sacred and ceremonial objects, whose significance is inseparable from their active role within living cultural practices rather than their status as historical specimens.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What does the author most likely suggest about emerging compromise frameworks such as long-term loans and joint stewardship agreements?", options: ["The author implies that they represent genuine progress toward redress but acknowledges that critics view them as preserving institutional control while avoiding substantive concessions on ownership", "The author is dismissive of these frameworks, arguing that they are legally incoherent and that only full repatriation with transfer of legal title constitutes an ethically defensible outcome", "The author regards them as fully satisfactory solutions that appropriately balance the interests of holding institutions and origin communities without requiring either side to make significant concessions", "The author suggests that these frameworks are likely to become the dominant global standard for resolving repatriation disputes within the next decade as holding institutions face increasing regulatory pressure"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-163", num: 163, title: "Đề 163", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Understanding Obesity", paragraphs: ["Obesity is a medical condition in which a person carries an amount of body fat that is likely to cause health problems. Doctors often measure it using a simple calculation called body mass index, or BMI, which compares a person's weight to their height. Someone with a BMI over a certain number is generally classified as obese. Over the past several decades, the number of people living with obesity has risen sharply in almost every part of the world, including many countries where being overweight was once considered rare.", "Several factors contribute to this rise. Diets in many countries now include far more processed food, sugary drinks, and fast food than in the past, and these products are often cheaper and more convenient than fresh, home-cooked meals. At the same time, many jobs and daily routines require much less physical movement than they used to, since machines, cars, and computers have replaced many tasks that once involved walking or manual labor. Genetics also plays a role: some people are more likely than others to gain weight easily, even when they eat similar amounts of food. This tendency does not mean lifestyle changes are useless, however, since environment still strongly influences outcomes.", "Obesity is linked to a wide range of health problems. People with obesity face a higher risk of developing heart disease, type 2 diabetes, and high blood pressure than people of average weight. Extra body weight also puts additional strain on joints, particularly the knees and hips, which can lead to pain and reduced mobility over time. Beyond physical health, obesity can affect mental wellbeing as well, since people living with the condition sometimes experience unfair treatment or negative comments from others, which can lead to stress, low self-esteem, or depression.", "Reducing obesity rates usually requires changes at both the individual and the community level. On a personal level, eating more fruits, vegetables, and whole foods while cutting back on sugar and processed snacks can help maintain a healthier weight, along with regular physical activity such as walking, cycling, or sports. Governments in some countries have also introduced policies to support healthier choices, including taxes on sugary drinks, clearer nutrition labels on packaged food, and programs that encourage physical education in schools. Experts generally agree that no single solution works alone; lasting change usually requires a combination of these approaches.", "Obesity remains a serious public health challenge, but it is not an unsolvable one. With better access to healthy food, more opportunities for physical activity, and continued public education, many health experts believe that current trends can eventually be slowed or reversed, even if progress is likely to take many years."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Obesity is caused only by eating too much sugary food.", "Obesity is a growing global health problem with multiple causes and solutions.", "Every country in the world now has extremely high obesity rates.", "Genetics is the single biggest cause of rising obesity rates."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do doctors commonly use to measure obesity?", options: ["Blood pressure readings taken during a physical exam.", "Body mass index, which compares weight to height.", "The total number of calories eaten each day.", "The number of obese people has risen for decades."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what health problem can extra body weight cause in the knees and hips?", options: ["Permanent bone fractures requiring immediate surgery.", "A higher risk of heart disease and diabetes.", "Complete loss of movement within a few years.", "Pain and reduced mobility over time."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one government policy mentioned to encourage healthier choices?", options: ["Banning fast food restaurants completely nationwide.", "Placing taxes on sugary soft drinks.", "Free gym memberships for all citizens.", "Clearer nutrition labels on packaged food."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cause of rising obesity rates?", options: ["Less physical movement in daily routines and jobs.", "A lack of public awareness about healthy eating.", "More processed food and sugary drinks in diets.", "Genetic differences in how easily people gain weight."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"convenient\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["scarce", "customary", "costly", "handy"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"strain\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["weight", "damage", "stain", "pressure"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"This tendency\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["lifestyle changes", "environmental influence", "similar food amounts", "easier weight gain"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about solving the obesity problem?", options: ["Extra body weight can strain the knees and hips.", "Rising obesity rates caused governments to invent nutrition labels first.", "Government taxes alone will completely eliminate obesity within years.", "Long-term progress will likely need both personal and government action."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward solving the obesity problem?", options: ["Realistic but hopeful that gradual progress is possible.", "Certain that obesity will disappear very soon.", "Convinced that genetics makes change impossible.", "Focused only on criticizing fast food companies."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Job-Hopping Among Younger Workers Today", paragraphs: ["Although changing jobs frequently was once viewed as a mark of instability on a résumé, that perception has shifted considerably among younger workers today, many of whom treat moving between employers every few years as a normal, even strategic, part of building a career. Surveys conducted across multiple countries suggest that younger employees now change jobs substantially more often than workers did at the same career stage a generation ago.", "One of the most consistently cited reasons for this shift is compensation. Numerous workforce studies have found that employees who switch employers typically receive larger salary increases than those who stay and wait for internal raises or promotions, a gap that has, if anything, widened in competitive labor markets. For many younger workers navigating rising living costs, this financial incentive alone can make switching jobs considerably more attractive than loyalty to a single employer.", "That compensation alone cannot fully explain the trend, however, becomes clear once workplace culture and personal values enter the picture. Younger employees frequently report that they are willing to leave positions offering competitive pay but lacking flexibility, a sense of purpose, or opportunities for skill development. Where earlier generations sometimes tolerated dissatisfaction for the sake of stability, many younger workers appear less willing to remain in roles that no longer feel personally or professionally rewarding.", "From an employer's perspective, however, frequent turnover carries real costs. Recruiting, hiring, and training replacement staff is expensive and time-consuming, and organizations with especially high turnover often struggle to maintain institutional knowledge and team cohesion . In response, many companies have introduced retention strategies such as flexible working arrangements, clearer paths for internal promotion, and more frequent salary reviews. These measures , however, appear to vary considerably in effectiveness depending on industry and company size.", "The broader economic context has also played a role in enabling this pattern. In periods when the labor market favors job seekers, employees have more leverage to negotiate better terms elsewhere, and the perceived risk of leaving a stable position decreases accordingly. Remote and hybrid work arrangements, which expanded considerably in recent years, have further widened the pool of available opportunities for many workers, since geographic location no longer limits access to certain jobs as strictly as it once did.", "Not all observers view this trend favorably, however. Some career researchers caution that excessive job-hopping, particularly early in a career, may limit opportunities to develop the deep, specialized expertise that comes only from sustained experience within a single organization. Others note that certain employers, despite public statements embracing flexibility, still view frequent job changes on a résumé with some suspicion during hiring decisions, suggesting the shift in perception may be less complete than survey data alone would suggest.", "Job-hopping among younger workers, then, appears to reflect a genuine shift in how career success is defined, shaped by financial incentives, changing values, and a labor market that increasingly rewards mobility, even as questions remain about its long-term costs and whether employer attitudes have caught up as fully as the surveys imply."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Employers now fully welcome and reward frequent job changes without hesitation.", "All younger employees quit their jobs within the first year.", "Younger workers switch jobs mainly to escape unpleasant coworkers.", "Younger workers increasingly change jobs for varied reasons, though the trend has real trade-offs."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what have workforce studies found about employees who switch employers?", options: ["Recruiting and training replacement staff is expensive and slow.", "They are usually rehired by their previous employer eventually.", "They typically receive smaller raises than long-term employees do.", "They typically receive larger salary increases than those who stay."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what retention strategies have some companies introduced in response to turnover?", options: ["Mandatory long-term contracts preventing employees from leaving.", "Reduced salaries to discourage frequent job changes.", "High turnover can hurt institutional knowledge and cohesion.", "More flexible arrangements and clearer promotion paths."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what concern do some career researchers raise about excessive job-hopping?", options: ["It always leads to significantly lower lifetime earnings.", "Some employers still view frequent changes with suspicion.", "It prevents workers from ever finding a stable job.", "It may limit development of deep, specialized expertise."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason younger workers change jobs more often?", options: ["Encouragement from parents to explore multiple career paths.", "A desire for more flexibility and purpose at work.", "Switching employers always guarantees a larger salary increase.", "The complete disappearance of employer loyalty as a concept."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"cohesion\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["unity", "productivity", "adhesion", "conflict"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"leverage\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["influence", "control", "leisure", "salary"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"These measures\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["internal promotion paths", "company size differences", "the new retention strategies", "institutional knowledge loss"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the primary drivers of job-hopping among younger workers, according to the passage?", options: ["Frequent turnover is costly and time-consuming for most employers.", "Salary alone completely explains why younger workers change jobs often.", "A desire for purpose caused companies to raise salaries first.", "Both financial and non-financial factors likely drive the trend together."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in this passage?", options: ["To convince readers that job-hopping is always beneficial.", "To explain a workplace trend from multiple perspectives.", "To assert that traditional career loyalty is now obsolete.", "To criticize employers for offering low starting salaries."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Social Media Across Generations: A Shifting Landscape of Use", paragraphs: ["Although social media platforms are often discussed as though they attract a single, homogeneous audience, usage patterns in fact differ substantially across age groups, shaped as much by when a generation first encountered the internet as by any inherent generational trait. Surveys consistently find that younger users, roughly those born after the mid-1990s, spend more time on social media daily than older generations, though the gap has narrowed somewhat as platforms have broadened their reach.", "Platform choice varies considerably by age as well. Younger users tend to favor short-form video and image-based platforms, drawn to content that can be consumed quickly and often algorithmically tailored to individual interests. Older users, particularly those over fifty, are more likely to rely on platforms originally designed around text-based updates, using them primarily to stay in touch with family or follow local news, rather than to discover new content from strangers.", "That younger users treat social media primarily as a source of entertainment and self-expression, while older users treat it primarily as a tool for maintaining existing relationships, reflects a broader difference in how each generation approaches the technology. Younger users are considerably more likely to report using social media to discover new music, follow influencers, or participate in trending challenges, whereas older users more often describe checking in on grandchildren or simply keeping up with acquaintances they would otherwise lose contact with.", "Social media has also become an important, if contested, source of news across generations, though the pattern differs by age. Younger users increasingly report getting news primarily through social platforms rather than traditional outlets, often encountering headlines passively while scrolling for other content. Older users, while also consuming social media news, more frequently supplement it with traditional sources such as television or print, and surveys suggest they are somewhat more skeptical of information encountered exclusively online.", "Attitudes toward privacy also diverge . Older users, several studies suggest, tend to be more cautious about sharing personal information publicly, having formed their habits before social platforms became as widespread as they are today. Younger users, having grown up with social media as a constant presence, often display more comfort sharing personal details online, though researchers caution that this apparent comfort does not necessarily mean younger users are less concerned about privacy in principle.", "The relationship between social media use and mental wellbeing remains one of the most actively debated questions in this area, and findings vary considerably depending on how usage is measured and which age group is studied. Some studies link heavy use among younger users to increased anxiety and lower life satisfaction. Other researchers, however, argue that correlation studies cannot establish that social media use actually causes these outcomes rather than simply coinciding with them. Effects on older users have received comparatively less research attention.", "Rather than a single technology producing a single effect, social media therefore functions differently depending on who is using it and why. Any policy or design response aimed at improving how the technology is used will likely need to account for this generational variation rather than treating all users as a uniform group."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Social media usage patterns and effects vary considerably across generations.", "Older users are now the primary drivers of social media trends.", "Heavy social media use always causes anxiety in every age group.", "Younger users only use social media for entertainment purposes."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what type of platforms do younger users tend to favor?", options: ["Text-based platforms designed for community groups.", "Platforms tailored algorithmically to individual interests.", "Platforms focused mainly on professional networking.", "Short-form video and image-based platforms."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do older users' news habits differ from younger users' habits?", options: ["Younger users often encounter headlines while scrolling passively.", "Older users more often supplement it with traditional sources.", "Older users rely exclusively on social media for news.", "Older users trust online information more than younger users do."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do researchers caution about younger users' comfort sharing personal information online?", options: ["Older users formed cautious habits before platforms were widespread.", "It proves that younger users have no privacy concerns at all.", "It does not necessarily mean they care less about privacy.", "It means younger users deliberately reject all privacy protections."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way generations differ in their social media use?", options: ["Which types of platforms each generation tends to prefer.", "How much they always trust every piece of online news.", "How much money each generation spends on premium features.", "Whether social media has been proven to cause anxiety everywhere."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"homogeneous\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["uniform", "homegrown", "diverse", "anonymous"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"diverge\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["improve", "collide", "differ", "diverse"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"these outcomes\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["heavy social media use", "correlation study findings", "older users' research attention", "anxiety and low satisfaction"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the overall relationship between social media use and wellbeing, according to the passage?", options: ["Low life satisfaction among young people caused heavier social media use.", "Older users generally trust online information less than younger users.", "The overall relationship likely varies and remains scientifically unsettled for now.", "Heavy social media use has been proven to cause anxiety in youth."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in this passage?", options: ["To argue that older users should adopt younger users' habits.", "To prove that social media definitely harms mental health.", "To describe how generational differences shape social media use.", "To insist that all generations use social media identically."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Nanotechnology: Engineering at the Scale of Atoms", paragraphs: ["Not since the invention of the transistor has a single technological concept promised to reshape as many industries simultaneously as nanotechnology, the science of manipulating matter at scales roughly between one and one hundred nanometers — a range so small that a single strand of human hair is tens of thousands of nanometers wide by comparison. At this scale, materials frequently exhibit physical and chemical properties markedly different from their behavior in bulk form, a phenomenon that underlies much of nanotechnology's practical promise.", "This shift in behavior arises largely because, as particles shrink, an increasingly large proportion of their atoms sit at or near the surface rather than buried within the bulk material, dramatically increasing reactivity relative to particle size. Below a certain threshold, quantum mechanical effects also begin to influence how particles interact with light and electricity, effects generally negligible at larger scales but sometimes dominant at the nanoscale. Researchers have exploited both phenomena to engineer materials with properties that would be difficult to achieve through conventional manufacturing.", "In medicine, nanotechnology has generated particular excitement around targeted drug delivery, in which nanoscale particles are engineered to carry medication directly to diseased cells, such as tumors, while largely sparing healthy tissue. Several such treatments have received regulatory approval and are now used clinically, though the field has, by most accounts, advanced more slowly than early predictions suggested, partly because designing particles that reliably reach their intended target inside a complex biological system has proven considerably more difficult than initial results implied.", "In electronics, nanotechnology has been central to the continued miniaturization of computer chips, allowing manufacturers to fit exponentially more transistors onto a single chip than earlier techniques permitted. This progress , however, appears to be approaching physical limits, since transistor components have shrunk to a scale at which quantum effects begin to interfere with reliable switching behavior, prompting researchers to explore alternative architectures that might sustain performance gains once conventional miniaturization reaches its ceiling.", "Materials engineered at the nanoscale have also found their way into everyday products, from stain-resistant fabrics to sunscreens that use nanoscale particles to block ultraviolet light more effectively than earlier formulations. Carbon nanotubes and related nanomaterials, meanwhile, exhibit tensile strength many times greater than steel at a fraction of the weight, properties that have generated interest in applications ranging from aerospace to sports equipment, even though manufacturing these materials at commercial scale remains considerably more expensive than conventional alternatives.", "That nanotechnology's benefits have not come without concern owes largely to uncertainty about how nanoscale particles behave once released into biological or environmental systems. Because their small size allows some nanoparticles to cross barriers, such as the blood-brain barrier, that would block larger particles, researchers have raised questions about potential health effects that remain incompletely understood. Regulatory frameworks in many countries have struggled to keep pace, in part because testing methods designed for conventional chemicals do not always translate straightforwardly to nanoscale materials.", "Nanotechnology therefore occupies an uncertain middle ground between transformative promise and unresolved risk, having already delivered concrete advances in several industries while leaving significant scientific and regulatory questions unanswered. Whether the technology's next generation of applications arrives as quickly as early enthusiasts predicted will likely depend as much on resolving these open questions as on further scientific breakthroughs."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Nanotechnology has completely replaced conventional manufacturing in every industry.", "Nanotechnology offers real advances across industries but faces unresolved risks.", "Regulatory concerns have already stopped most nanotechnology research entirely.", "Nanotechnology is used mainly to make stronger sunscreen products."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do materials at the nanoscale often behave differently from their bulk form?", options: ["They can be engineered for greater strength and conductivity.", "They contain fewer atoms than larger materials overall.", "More of their atoms sit near the surface.", "They are always heavier than their bulk equivalents."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why has targeted drug delivery advanced more slowly than early predictions suggested?", options: ["Government funding for medical nanotechnology research was cut entirely.", "Several such treatments have already received regulatory approval.", "Reliably reaching the intended target proved more difficult than expected.", "Patients refused to participate in nanotechnology drug trials."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what physical limitation is chip miniaturization beginning to approach?", options: ["Manufacturers fitting more transistors onto a single chip.", "Rising consumer demand for larger, not smaller, devices.", "Quantum effects interfering with reliable switching behavior.", "A complete shortage of silicon raw materials worldwide."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an application of nanotechnology?", options: ["Eliminating quantum effects entirely from chip design.", "Making every sunscreen completely block all ultraviolet light.", "Purifying ocean water on an industrial scale.", "Delivering medication directly to targeted diseased cells."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"negligible\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["insignificant", "measurable", "neglected", "dangerous"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"translate\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["apply", "convert", "transmit", "interpret"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"This progress\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["increasing transistor density gains", "physical limits reached", "earlier manufacturing techniques", "a single computer chip"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the overall pace of nanotechnology's real-world impact, according to the passage?", options: ["Regulatory uncertainty caused researchers to abandon nanoscale drug delivery.", "Progress has been real but slower than early predictions suggested.", "Medical applications have now completely fulfilled their early promise.", "Carbon nanotubes exhibit far greater tensile strength than steel."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in this passage?", options: ["To present a balanced view of nanotechnology's promise and risks.", "To assert confidently that nanotechnology risks are already fully understood.", "To prove that quantum computing will soon replace all chips.", "To argue that sunscreen manufacturers should stop using nanoparticles."], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
];
